11. Mathematical Development Roadmap

To convert this framework into a formal theorem:

Stage 1

Define:

\Phi \in H^2

with admissibility.

Stage 2

Prove compactness in Sobolev embeddings.

Stage 3

Show curvature boundedness.

Stage 4

Derive contradiction under assumed singularity.

Stage 5

Extend to weak solutions.

⸻

12. Conclusion

Phase Theory proposes that the Navier–Stokes regularity problem is fundamentally a problem of phase topology, not uncontrolled nonlinear amplification.

Its core claim is:

Singularities are forbidden because coherent phase manifolds cannot undergo topological collapse while maintaining finite phase energy.

If formalized rigorously, this may establish a new route toward resolving the Navier–Stokes problem.

At present, this remains a Phase-Theoretic research conjecture, not an accepted proof.